- Story & Context: Long after the original eclipse troupe vanished into police warrants and tidal fog, mudlarks scavenging the Deptford waterline still discover tarnished silver medallions stamped with the band's signature crowned-skull emblem. Seasoned dockworkers swear that whenever thick storm clouds blot out the midday sun, percussive acoustic chords and ghostly cuíca wails vibrate up through the rotting timber pilings of the old wharf. An eccentric retired lighterman named Arthur claims he saw the entire band deliberately step off the pier into the freezing black river the second the eclipse ended, leaving behind only their echoing groove and one final phrase carved into the pier timber: "Pay the boatman with a silver disc and dance your shoes to bone."
